================================================================================
Where the facts come from
================================================================================

Every rule this tool depends on was checked against a primary source. Where a
community source disagreed with the client, the client won.

  The bitmap provider's codec — `file` required, `height` optional and defaulting
  to 8, `ascent` required, `chars` rows validated to equal length, and
  `ascent > height` rejected outright — was read from the client's font provider
  code (BitmapProvider.Definition), via a publicly available decompilation used
  as read-only evidence. No decompiled code is reproduced here.

  The advance formula `(int)(0.5 + inkWidth * pixelScale) + 1`, the cell division
  `imageWidth / columns` by `imageHeight / rows`, and `getActualGlyphWidth`
  scanning right to left for the first column with a non-zero alpha come from the
  same loader.

  `getTop() = 7 - getBearingTop()` and `getBottom() = getTop() + pixelHeight /
  oversample`, which is what makes the vertical placement one subtraction, come
  from the glyph bitmap interface (GlyphBitmap) in the same tree.

  The 256-pixel atlas page and the one-page-then-give-up stitching behaviour that
  turns an oversized cell into the missing-glyph box come from FontTexture and
  GlyphStitcher.

  That fonts merge across packs rather than override — every pack's copy of a font
  id is loaded and its providers concatenated — comes from FontManager, and is
  observable in AmberWat/NegativeSpaceFont, whose font/default.json lists three
  providers of its own and does not stop normal text from rendering.

  The `space` provider's advances being floats rather than integers comes from its
  codec (Codec.FLOAT). minecraft.wiki states they must be integers; that is
  incorrect for current versions, and NegativeSpaceFont ships fractional values.

  The container title anchor — 8 pixels right, 6 pixels down from the window's
  top-left corner, drawn in 0x404040 without a shadow — comes from
  AbstractContainerScreen, where it is unchanged from 1.21.1 through 26.2.

  The pack.mcmeta rules, including the format-64 boundary between the
  `supported_formats` era and the `min_format`/`max_format` era, the requirement
  that both new fields appear together, the rejection of `supported_formats` above
  64, and `pack_format` needing to be at least 15 in a multi-version pack, come
  from the pack metadata codec (PackFormat.IntermediaryFormat#validate).

  The version-to-format table in PackFormats.kt lists releases only and was
  derived from the version summary published by misode/mcmeta
  (https://github.com/misode/mcmeta), which mirrors Mojang's version manifest.
  The numbers are facts about the game; no assets from that repository, and no
  Mojang assets, are included here.

  ItemsAdder's and Nexo's documentation independently corroborate the ascent
  limit and the 256-pixel cap, and are quoted in the README as evidence that the
  problem this tool solves is the one their users hit.

================================================================================
Prior art
================================================================================

None of these projects' code is used. Reading them shaped what this tool does and,
more usefully, what it does not try to do.

  unnamed/creative              MIT           https://github.com/unnamed/creative
                                A resource pack object model and serialiser for
                                the JVM, actively maintained. The right dependency
                                for a plugin. It has no CLI, no codepoint
                                allocator and no layout solver, which is the part
                                this tool exists for.

  Brikster/glyphs               Apache-2.0    https://github.com/Brikster/glyphs
                                Absolutely positioned textures as a library, and
                                the closest existing statement of the problem.
                                Unmaintained since 2023.

  AmberWat/NegativeSpaceFont    CC-BY-4.0     https://github.com/AmberWat/NegativeSpaceFont
                                The reference negative-space pack, and the source
                                of the convention of allocating well away from the
                                private use area everything else crowds into.
                                glyphsmith generates equivalent providers from the
                                codec instead of shipping this pack, so no
                                attribution requirement reaches your pack.

  toxicity188/BetterHud         MIT           https://github.com/toxicity188/BetterHud
                                A Kotlin HUD framework with its own pack
                                generator, and the closest stylistic reference for
                                generating packs from the JVM.

  ComunidadAylas/PackSquash     AGPL-3.0      https://github.com/ComunidadAylas/PackSquash
                                Pack optimisation, done properly. Run it after
                                this, as a separate program.

  Oraxen, Nexo, ItemsAdder, Sunscreen
                                Various, some closed or source-available with
                                restrictions. Each solves glyph management at
                                runtime inside a plugin. Their documented key
                                names and reserved codepoint ranges informed the
                                defaults here — in particular, allocating from
                                plane 15 to stay out of their way. No code from
                                any of them is present, and none of their config
                                formats is emitted.

  misode/mcmeta                 no licence file — treated as read-only reference
                                https://github.com/misode/mcmeta
                                Used to verify the shape of real vanilla font
                                files and to derive the version-to-format table.

